(* Editor — the editor's state and the keymap over it.
 *
 * A program-relative module of examples/battallion. Pure: no process, no receive, no descriptor,
 * no file. The kernel process (Session.trp) threads a `state` record through `onKey`, `onResize`
 * and `onWritten`, hands `frameOf` to the renderer, and performs the one effect this module can
 * ask for — writing the buffer out — on its behalf.
 *
 * STATE IS A RECORD, NOT A DATATYPE, so that later stages can add fields without breaking the
 * matches written against it (open record patterns, `{a = p, ..}`). The fields:
 *
 *   buffer   the text, a lib/Rope.trp rope
 *   path     the file the text came from and `:w` writes back to
 *   top      the buffer line drawn on the terminal's first row
 *   line     the cursor's buffer line, counting from zero
 *   col      the cursor's column within that line, counting from zero
 *   cols     the terminal's width, in code units
 *   rows     the terminal's height, in rows, status line included
 *   mode     NORMAL, INSERT or COMMAND
 *   dirty    whether the buffer differs from what was last read or written
 *   undo     the states `u` walks back through, newest first
 *   cmd      the command line being typed, without its leading colon
 *   status   a transient message for the status line; "" when there is none
 *   req      what the keymap is asking the kernel to do, REQ_NONE when nothing
 *
 * THE CURSOR MAY SIT ONE PAST THE LAST CHARACTER of its line, i.e. `col` ranges over
 * `0..length`, not `0..length-1`, in every mode. Insert mode needs the position, and a vi-style
 * normal mode that stops one earlier is a keymap decision — the keymap being what stage 6
 * extracts. One consequence is visible: leaving insert mode does not pull the cursor left the way
 * vi does, so `i`, some text, Esc leaves the cursor after the text rather than on its last
 * character.
 *
 * THE COLUMN IS NOT REMEMBERED ACROSS VERTICAL MOTIONS. Moving down from column 40 onto a short
 * line puts the cursor at that line's end, and moving down again keeps it there rather than
 * returning to 40. The remembered "goal column" that vi has is a keymap feature; it belongs with
 * the motions plugin of stage 6, not in the state the kernel owns.
 *
 * TABS ARE NOT EXPANDED. A tab in the text is one code unit for both the column arithmetic and
 * the renderer's truncation, and the terminal draws it as a jump to the next tab stop, so the
 * cursor lands left of where the character appears. Expanding tabs is a rendering-width question
 * — the same one wide characters raise (Screen.trp's header) — and the mvp editor does not
 * measure display width.
 *
 * OFFSETS ARE CODE UNITS, as lib/Rope.trp's are, and a character outside the basic multilingual
 * plane is two of them. The two places where that would show — `x` and Backspace — read the
 * surrogate pair and remove both halves, so no edit can leave half a character in the buffer.
 * Everything else counts units: `$` on a line of astral characters stops past the last one's
 * high half, and the column shown in the status line counts units too.
 *
 * NORMAL MODE. `h j k l` and the arrows move by one; `0` and Home go to the line's start, `$` and
 * End to its end; PageUp/PageDown and CTRL-b/CTRL-f move by one screen. `i` enters insert mode,
 * `o` opens a line below and enters it, `x` deletes the character under the cursor, `u` undoes,
 * `:` starts a command line. `q` and CTRL-c quit, refusing on a modified buffer exactly as `:q`
 * does. CTRL-c quits because in raw mode it arrives as the byte 0x03 with no signal raised, so
 * without a binding the habit of pressing it would do nothing at all. Every other key is ignored.
 *
 * INSERT MODE. A printable key inserts itself, whatever it is: the payload of `KEY_PRINT` is one
 * code point as a string and it goes into the buffer as it stands, so a multibyte character typed
 * or pasted is stored as typed. Enter splits the line, Tab inserts a tab, Backspace deletes the
 * character to the left and joins with the line above when there is none, Delete removes the
 * character under the cursor, Esc returns to normal mode. Motions work in insert mode too.
 *
 * COMMAND MODE. `:` opens it. Printable keys extend the command, Backspace shortens it and
 * leaves the mode when the command is empty — as backspacing over the colon does in vi — Esc
 * cancels, Enter runs it. The commands are `w`, `q`, `q!`, `wq` and `x` (a synonym for `wq`);
 * anything else is reported in the status line. The mvp has no ranges, no arguments and no
 * `:w <other-path>`.
 *
 * QUITTING A MODIFIED BUFFER IS REFUSED, by `q`, by CTRL-c and by `:q` alike: the status line
 * says so and the session stays up. `:q!` is the override, `:wq` the other way out. The mvp key
 * set names both `:q` and `:q!`, which is only a distinction if `:q` can refuse.
 *
 * UNDO IS PER KEYSTROKE and unlimited. Every key that changes the buffer first pushes the whole
 * previous state — rope, cursor, dirty flag — onto `undo`, and `u` pops one. Two consequences are
 * deliberate. Typing a word and undoing it takes as many `u`s as there were letters, where vi
 * would undo the whole insertion; grouping keystrokes into changes is a keymap feature and would
 * belong with stage 6's plugins. And the history costs almost nothing: a rope shares every node
 * the edit did not touch with its predecessor, so a snapshot is a handful of nodes rather than a
 * copy of the text — which is the reason the buffer is a persistent structure in the first place.
 * The dirty flag is part of the snapshot, so undoing every change back to the file's contents
 * also clears it, and `u` on an empty history is a status message rather than an error.
 *
 * THE STATUS LINE TAKES THE BOTTOM ROW, so the text occupies `rows - 1` of them; `textRows` is
 * where that is decided. In command mode the row is the command line; otherwise it names the
 * file, flags a modified buffer with `[+]`, names the mode, gives the cursor's one-based line and
 * column, and carries a transient message when there is one. A message lives until the next
 * keystroke: `onKey` clears it before anything else.
 *
 * THE KEYMAP CANNOT WRITE A FILE — it is pure, and the ROOT authority a write needs never leaves
 * the supervisor's frame (Session.trp). So `:w` sets `req` to `REQ_WRITE` instead, the kernel
 * sees the request in the state it gets back, asks the supervisor to write, and feeds the outcome
 * to `onWritten`. `REQ_WRITE`'s payload says whether to quit after a successful write, which is
 * what makes `:wq` one request rather than two. A refused write clears the request, leaves the
 * buffer modified, and puts the reason in the status line.
 *)

import String
import List
import Rope
import "./Key"

datatype mode = NORMAL | INSERT | COMMAND

(* What the keymap asks the kernel to do. The payload of `REQ_WRITE` is whether a successful
 * write ends the session. *)
datatype request = REQ_NONE | REQ_WRITE of bool

let (*--------------------------------  Construction  --------------------------------*)

    (** The state a session starts in: the whole buffer, cursor at the start, nothing modified. *)
    fun init (buffer, path, cols, rows) =
        { buffer = buffer, path = path, top = 0, line = 0, col = 0, cols = cols, rows = rows
        , mode = NORMAL, dirty = false, undo = [], cmd = "", status = "", req = REQ_NONE }

    (*----------------------------------  Messages  ----------------------------------*)

    (* The confirmation of a successful write is not here: the supervisor is what writes, so it is
     * what knows whether the write succeeded and what to say when it did not. *)
    val modifiedMessage = "no write since last change (add ! to override)"
    val oldestMessage   = "already at the oldest change"

    (*----------------------------------  Queries  -----------------------------------*)

    (** The number of terminal rows that show text: every row but the status line's. A terminal
     *  of one row keeps that row for the text and has no status line — `frameOf` agrees. *)
    fun textRows st = if st.rows > 1 then st.rows - 1 else st.rows

    (** Whether the terminal is tall enough for a status line. *)
    fun hasStatus st = st.rows > 1

    fun lastLine st = Rope.lineCount st.buffer - 1

    fun lineLength (st, l) = String.size (Rope.lineAt (st.buffer, l))

    (** The whole buffer as one string, for the kernel to hand the supervisor to write. *)
    fun contents st = Rope.text st.buffer

    (*----------------------------------  Motions  -----------------------------------*)

    fun clampLine (st, l) =
        let val last = lastLine st
        in if l < 0 then 0 else if l > last then last else l
        end

    fun clampCol (st, l, c) =
        let val n = lineLength (st, l)
        in if c < 0 then 0 else if c > n then n else c
        end

    (** The top line that keeps `line` on the screen, moving the viewport by the least it can. *)
    fun scrollTo (st, line) =
        let val h = textRows st
        in      if line < st.top          then line
           else if line >= st.top + h     then line - h + 1
           else st.top
        end

    (** The state with the cursor at `(l, c)`, both clamped, and the viewport following it. *)
    fun moveTo (st, l, c) =
        let val l' = clampLine (st, l)
            val c' = clampCol (st, l', c)
        in { st with line = l', col = c', top = scrollTo (st, l') }
        end

    (*----------------------------------  Editing  -----------------------------------*)

    (* The surrogate ranges. A character outside the basic multilingual plane is stored as a high
     * unit followed by a low one, and an edit that took only one of them would leave text that no
     * longer decodes. *)
    val surHighLow = 0xd800   val surHighHigh = 0xdbff
    val surLowLow  = 0xdc00   val surLowHigh  = 0xdfff

    (** The code unit at offset `i`, or 0 outside the rope. *)
    fun unitAt (r, i) = String.subCode (Rope.charAt (r, i), 0)

    (** The offset the cursor sits at. *)
    fun offsetOf st = Rope.positionToOffset (st.buffer, st.line, st.col)

    (** How many code units the character ending at `off` occupies. *)
    fun unitsBefore (st, off) =
        if off <= 1 then 1
        else let val lo = unitAt (st.buffer, off - 1)
                 val hi = unitAt (st.buffer, off - 2)
             in if lo >= surLowLow  andalso lo <= surLowHigh
                   andalso hi >= surHighLow andalso hi <= surHighHigh
                then 2 else 1
             end

    (** How many code units the character starting at `off` occupies. *)
    fun unitsAt (st, off) =
        let val hi = unitAt (st.buffer, off)
        in if hi >= surHighLow andalso hi <= surHighHigh then 2 else 1
        end

    (** The state with `buffer` as its text, the cursor at code-unit offset `off`, marked
     *  modified and the viewport following the cursor. The cursor is derived from the offset
     *  rather than adjusted line by line, so a newline inserted or a line join lands it in the
     *  right place without a case for each. *)
    fun atOffset (st, buffer, off) =
        let val (l, c) = Rope.offsetToPosition (buffer, off)
            val st' = { st with buffer = buffer, line = l, col = c, dirty = true }
        in { st' with top = scrollTo (st', l) }
        end

    (** The state with its present buffer and cursor pushed onto the undo history. *)
    fun remember st =
        { st with undo = { buffer = st.buffer, line = st.line, col = st.col, dirty = st.dirty }
                         :: st.undo }

    (** `s` inserted at the cursor, which ends up after it. *)
    fun insertText (st, s) =
        let val off = offsetOf st
            val st1 = remember st
        in atOffset (st1, Rope.insert (st1.buffer, off, s), off + String.size s)
        end

    (** `n` code units removed from `off`, with the cursor left there. *)
    fun deleteAt (st, off, n) =
        if n <= 0 then st
        else let val st1 = remember st
             in atOffset (st1, Rope.delete (st1.buffer, off, n), off)
             end

    (** `x`: the character under the cursor, if the cursor is on one. The newline ending the line
     *  is not one — deleting past the end of a line would join it with the next, which `x` does
     *  not do in vi either. *)
    fun deleteChar st =
        let val off = offsetOf st
        in if st.col >= lineLength (st, st.line) then st
           else deleteAt (st, off, unitsAt (st, off))
        end

    (** Backspace: the character to the left, or the line break when there is none, so that
     *  backspacing at the start of a line joins it to the one above. *)
    fun backspace st =
        let val off = offsetOf st
        in if off <= 0 then st
           else let val n = unitsBefore (st, off)
                in deleteAt (st, off - n, n)
                end
        end

    (** `o`: an empty line below the cursor's, and insert mode on it. *)
    fun openBelow st =
        let val stop = Rope.positionToOffset (st.buffer, st.line, lineLength (st, st.line))
            val st1 = remember st
            val st2 = atOffset (st1, Rope.insert (st1.buffer, stop, "\n"), stop + 1)
        in { st2 with mode = INSERT }
        end

    (** `u`: the state before the last change, or a message when there is none. *)
    fun undo st =
        case st.undo of
             []        => { st with status = oldestMessage }
           | s :: rest =>
               let val st1 = { st with buffer = s.buffer, undo = rest, dirty = s.dirty }
               in moveTo (st1, s.line, s.col)
               end

    (*--------------------------------  The commands  --------------------------------*)

    (** Quitting: refused while the buffer is modified. *)
    fun quit st = if st.dirty then ({ st with status = modifiedMessage }, false) else (st, true)

    (** The command line, run. Returns `(state', quit)` like a key does; a command that writes
     *  quits through `onWritten` instead, once the write has succeeded. *)
    fun execute st =
        let val c   = st.cmd
            val st0 = { st with mode = NORMAL, cmd = "" }
        in      if c = "w"                then ({ st0 with req = REQ_WRITE false }, false)
           else if c = "wq" orelse c = "x" then ({ st0 with req = REQ_WRITE true }, false)
           else if c = "q"                then quit st0
           else if c = "q!"               then (st0, true)
           else ({ st0 with status = "not an editor command: " ^ c }, false)
        end

    (** The state after the kernel has written the buffer out. `ok` says whether the write
     *  succeeded and `message` is what to show — the reason on failure. A successful write clears
     *  the modified flag and, for `:wq`, ends the session; a failed one leaves the buffer
     *  modified and the session up. Either way the request is spent. *)
    fun onWritten (st, ok, message) =
        let val quitAfter = case st.req of REQ_WRITE q => q
                                         | _           => false
            val st' = { st with req = REQ_NONE, status = message
                              , dirty = if ok then false else st.dirty }
        in (st', ok andalso quitAfter)
        end

    (*----------------------------------  The keymap  --------------------------------*)

    (** The motions, shared by normal and insert mode. Any other key leaves the state alone. *)
    fun motion (st, KEY_LEFT)     = moveTo (st, st.line, st.col - 1)
      | motion (st, KEY_RIGHT)    = moveTo (st, st.line, st.col + 1)
      | motion (st, KEY_DOWN)     = moveTo (st, st.line + 1, st.col)
      | motion (st, KEY_UP)       = moveTo (st, st.line - 1, st.col)
      | motion (st, KEY_HOME)     = moveTo (st, st.line, 0)
      | motion (st, KEY_END)      = moveTo (st, st.line, lineLength (st, st.line))
      | motion (st, KEY_PAGEDOWN) = moveTo (st, st.line + textRows st, st.col)
      | motion (st, KEY_PAGEUP)   = moveTo (st, st.line - textRows st, st.col)
      | motion (st, _)            = st

    (** Normal mode. *)
    fun normalKey (st, KEY_PRINT c) =
             if c = "h"      then (moveTo (st, st.line, st.col - 1), false)
        else if c = "l"      then (moveTo (st, st.line, st.col + 1), false)
        else if c = "j"      then (moveTo (st, st.line + 1, st.col), false)
        else if c = "k"      then (moveTo (st, st.line - 1, st.col), false)
        else if c = "0"      then (moveTo (st, st.line, 0), false)
        else if c = "$"      then (moveTo (st, st.line, lineLength (st, st.line)), false)
        else if c = "i"      then ({ st with mode = INSERT }, false)
        else if c = "o"      then (openBelow st, false)
        else if c = "x"      then (deleteChar st, false)
        else if c = "u"      then (undo st, false)
        else if c = ":"      then ({ st with mode = COMMAND, cmd = "" }, false)
        else if c = "q"      then quit st
        else (st, false)
      | normalKey (st, KEY_CTRL c) =
             if c = "f" then (moveTo (st, st.line + textRows st, st.col), false)
        else if c = "b" then (moveTo (st, st.line - textRows st, st.col), false)
        else if c = "c" then quit st
        else (st, false)
      | normalKey (st, KEY_DELETE) = (deleteChar st, false)
      | normalKey (st, k)          = (motion (st, k), false)

    (** Insert mode. *)
    fun insertKey (st, KEY_PRINT c)   = (insertText (st, c), false)
      | insertKey (st, KEY_ENTER)     = (insertText (st, "\n"), false)
      | insertKey (st, KEY_TAB)       = (insertText (st, "\t"), false)
      | insertKey (st, KEY_BACKSPACE) = (backspace st, false)
      | insertKey (st, KEY_DELETE)    = (deleteChar st, false)
      | insertKey (st, KEY_ESC)       = ({ st with mode = NORMAL }, false)
      | insertKey (st, k)             = (motion (st, k), false)

    (** Command mode. *)
    fun commandKey (st, KEY_PRINT c) = ({ st with cmd = st.cmd ^ c }, false)
      | commandKey (st, KEY_ENTER)   = execute st
      | commandKey (st, KEY_ESC)     = ({ st with mode = NORMAL, cmd = "" }, false)
      | commandKey (st, KEY_BACKSPACE) =
          if st.cmd = "" then ({ st with mode = NORMAL }, false)
          else ({ st with cmd = String.substring (st.cmd, 0, String.size st.cmd - 1) }, false)
      | commandKey (st, KEY_CTRL c) =
          if c = "c" then ({ st with mode = NORMAL, cmd = "" }, false) else (st, false)
      | commandKey (st, _) = (st, false)

    (** `(state', quit)` for one key. `quit` is true when the key ends the session.
     *
     *  A transient status message is cleared here rather than where it is set, so every message
     *  is shown for exactly as long as it takes the user to press the next key. *)
    fun onKey (st0, k) =
        let val st = if st0.status = "" then st0 else { st0 with status = "" }
        in case st.mode of INSERT  => insertKey (st, k)
                         | COMMAND => commandKey (st, k)
                         | _       => normalKey (st, k)
        end

    (*----------------------------------  Resizing  ----------------------------------*)

    (** The state on a new terminal size. The cursor keeps its buffer position and the viewport
     *  is recomputed around it, so a shrink that would have left the cursor off-screen scrolls
     *  instead of losing it. *)
    fun onResize (st, cols, rows) =
        let val st' = { st with cols = cols, rows = rows }
        in moveTo (st', st'.line, st'.col)
        end

    (*-----------------------------------  Frames  -----------------------------------*)

    (** The bottom row: the command line while one is being typed, and otherwise the file, the
     *  modified flag, the mode, the cursor's one-based position and any transient message. *)
    fun statusLine st =
        case st.mode of
             COMMAND => ":" ^ st.cmd
           | m       =>
               let val flag = if st.dirty then " [+]" else ""
                   val name = case m of INSERT => "INSERT" | _ => "NORMAL"
                   val pos  = toString (st.line + 1) ^ "," ^ toString (st.col + 1)
                   val base = st.path ^ flag ^ "  " ^ name ^ "  " ^ pos
               in if st.status = "" then base else base ^ "  " ^ st.status
               end

    (** The visible lines, one per terminal row, and the cursor's position on the screen.
     *
     *  Rows past the end of the buffer are drawn as "~", the filler vi has used since the
     *  seventies to say "this row is not part of the file". The last row is the status line, and
     *  in command mode the cursor sits on it, after the command typed so far. *)
    fun frameOf st =
        let val h    = textRows st
            val last = lastLine st
            fun rowsFrom (i, acc) =
                if i >= h then List.reverse acc
                else let val l = st.top + i
                     in rowsFrom (i + 1, (if l > last then "~" else Rope.lineAt (st.buffer, l)) :: acc)
                     end
            val body  = rowsFrom (0, [])
            val lines = if hasStatus st then List.append body [statusLine st] else body
            val (curRow, curCol) =
                    case st.mode of
                         COMMAND => (h, String.size st.cmd + 1)
                       | _       => ( st.line - st.top
                                    , if st.col >= st.cols then st.cols - 1 else st.col )
        in (lines, curRow, curCol)
        end

in [ ("init", init)
   , ("textRows", textRows)
   , ("lastLine", lastLine)
   , ("lineLength", lineLength)
   , ("contents", contents)
   , ("moveTo", moveTo)
   , ("undo", undo)
   , ("onKey", onKey)
   , ("onWritten", onWritten)
   , ("onResize", onResize)
   , ("statusLine", statusLine)
   , ("frameOf", frameOf)
   ]
end
